Scott asks to finish the two checks left open in Theorem 7.4 — that the separated sum \(\mathcal{D}_0+\mathcal{D}_1\), enumerated by \(Z_0=\Delta_0\cup\Delta_1\), \(Z_{2n+1}=X_n^0\), \(Z_{2n+2}=X_n^1\), and the product \(\mathcal{D}_0\times\mathcal{D}_1\), enumerated by \(W_k=X_{p(k)}^0\cup X_{q(k)}^1\) along a pairing \(r\), satisfy Definition 7.1(i)–(ii), and that \(\mathrm{in}_i\), \(\mathrm{out}_i\), \(\mathrm{proj}_i^2\), \(f+g\), and \(f\times g\) are computable — and to prove the same effective givenness, with the corresponding combinators, for the smash \(D_0\otimes D_1\), the coalesced sum \(D_0\oplus D_1\), and the infinite iterate \(D^\infty\). `example_715` is only the second group: `iterSys_isEffectivelyGiven` gives a full computable presentation of `iterSys` on \(\mathbb{N}\times\alpha\), while `osum_isEffectivelyGivenS` and `smash_isEffectivelyGivenS` give Scott presentations (decidable intersection-equality and consistency, which is Definition 7.1) of the coalesced sum and the smash; the nonempty-neighbourhood hypotheses on the two factors are the side condition under which `osum` closes under intersection. The separated-sum enumeration and the pairing enumeration of the cartesian product are not in this closure, nor are \(\mathrm{in}_i\), \(\mathrm{out}_i\), \(\mathrm{proj}_i^2\), \(f+g\), or \(f\times g\). For the smash, `smashEnum` returns the product of the two indexed neighbourhoods when both are proper and the product of the two masters otherwise, `smashPresentation` decides intersection-equality by rewriting each code, through the properness deciders, as a product of effective indices and taking the conjunction of the two component decisions, and decides consistency by using the other code when either factor is improper and the pair of component witnesses when both are proper. `smashProj₀_isComputable` and `smashProj₁_isComputable` reduce the preimage inclusion to decidable inclusion at a primitive-recursive index, `smashPaired_isComputable` makes strict pairing r.e. by the improper-master clause or the two computable relations on the factors, and `smashMap_isComputable` does the same for the smash of two computable maps. For the coalesced sum, `osumEnum` injects a proper left or right neighbourhood according to the tag and returns the separated-sum master otherwise, `osumPresentation` normalizes codes by the properness deciders and reduces intersection-equality to the separated sum while consistency is the master disjunction or same-side component consistency (opposite tags meet emptily), and `osumInMapu2080_isComputable`, `osumOutMapu2080_isComputable`, and `osumMap_isComputable`, with the right-hand twins, are the injection, projection, and sum-of-maps combinators by tag, properness, and inclusion. For \(D^\infty\), `iterEnum` reads a finite list of fiber indices and uses the master index past that list, `iterEnum_inter_eq_iff` and `iterEnum_cons_iff` bound the fiberwise checks by the sum of the codes because `iterIdx_ge` makes every later fiber the master, `iterPresentation` tabulates the coordinate-wise intersection and is primitive recursive, and `projN_isComputable` is the projection combinator, reducing \(\pi_n\) to inclusion of the \(n\)th coded fiber. Inclusion itself is `incl_computable`, the biconditional \(X\subseteq Y\) iff \(X\cap Y=X\) recorded after Definition 7.1. The same closure also contains `iterIterEffectiveIso`, the mutually inverse computable reindexings `Fmap` and `Gmap` along pairing, which is the effectiveness of \(D^\infty\cong(D^\infty)^\infty\) and is not one of the three conjuncts of `example_715`. **Theorem:** partial — smash, coalesced sum, and \(D^\infty\) are effectively given and their projections, injections, pairings, and map combinators are computable, while the separated sum and cartesian product left open in 7.4 are not proved here. **Proof:** partial — the three similar constructs are checked by the enumerations and the index-wise decidability and r.e. arguments above; the odd/even sum enumeration and the pairing product enumeration of 7.4 are absent.
